# Canonical check set: the default `Checks` list from ZedThree/clang-tidy-review,
# the most-used clang-tidy CI action. The durable rule is that lint/format
# settings stay the popular, canonical ones (no hand-tuned deviation) - the C++
# style base is the Qt ecosystem (see .clang-format), and this check set is the
# action's default verbatim (modulo this comment).
#
# Tuning layer. The canonical category block below stays the base; the three
# `-<check>` exclusions and the one CheckOption are the standard, documented
# noise-tuning large projects carry on top of the popular defaults (Chromium/
# LLVM/Google all do this). Re-baseline procedure and rationale live in
# docs/building.md "clang-tidy exclusions". Re-run `cmake --build build --target
# tidy` and review the top remaining categories before adding another exclusion -
# the goal is a signal you would act on, not a clean sheet.
#
# - misc-include-cleaner
#     GStreamer/GES are umbrella-header libraries: `gst.h`, `pbutils.h` and the
#     GES `*.h` headers pull their symbols from transitively-included internal
#     headers, so "no header providing X is directly included" is the normal
#     (and required) include shape, not a defect. Hand-including GStreamer
#     internals breaks on every upstream header move. Norm: include the umbrella
#     header, exactly as the GStreamer docs prescribe.
# - readability-identifier-length
#     The tree uses idiomatic short names - `id`, `ok`, `in`, `it`, `ec`, `c`,
#     `a`/`b` (comparison params), `j` (loop index), `at` - that the 3-char
#     minimum rejects wholesale. These are the domain's own words, not laziness.
#     The per-kind minimums cannot separate them from real noise without an
#     arbitrary ignore list, so exclusion is cleaner than a hand-tuned floor.
#     Norm: descriptive names except where the short form is the domain's word.
# - cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-avoid-unchecked-container-access
#     A blanket ban on `operator[]`; the tree indexes vectors/maps in loops and
#     static lookup tables whose index is in-bounds by construction. Norm:
#     `operator[]` with a known-safe index, `.at()` only where a bounds check is
#     the point. LLVM and Google both disable this check.
#
# CheckOption (not an exclusion - the check still runs and still catches the
# real encapsulation smell):
# - misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes.IgnoreClassesWithAllMemberVariablesBeingPublic
#     The tree's value types (`Ease`, `Key`, `Animation`, the API request DTOs)
#     are all-public-data structs ported from Rust, deliberately combining data
#     with a small helper (`operator==`, `apply`, `tidy`). The option silences
#     only classes whose member variables are *all* public; a class that mixes
#     public and private members is still flagged, which is exactly the smell
#     the check exists to catch.
Checks: >
  -*,performance-*,readability-*,bugprone-*,clang-analyzer-*,cppcoreguidelines-*,mpi-*,misc-*,
  -misc-include-cleaner,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-avoid-unchecked-container-access
CheckOptions:
  - key: misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes.IgnoreClassesWithAllMemberVariablesBeingPublic
    value: 'true'
WarningsAsErrors: ''
HeaderFilterRegex: '(src|tests)/.*'
FormatStyle: file
